 625: /**
 626:  * THE TWO-MEMBERSHIP GIVEN, provisioned once for AT-001.16 and AT-001.36 — and HOW it is reached is
 627:  * part of what those greens claim, so it is written down here rather than buried in a helper name.
 628:  *
 629:  * ONE ACCOUNT, THREE ORGANISATIONS, TWO MEMBERSHIP ROWS:
 630:  *   * A — created by the PRODUCT path. `complete_signup` writes the organisation and seats the
 631:  *     caller as its `admin`, inside one transaction, exactly as an NGO signup does.
 632:  *   * B — created by the OPERATOR with NO membership row, and the actor is then granted B's single
 633:  *     seat as `member`.
 634:  *   * C — created by the OPERATOR and left unseated, so the actor holds no membership in it at all.
 635:  *
 636:  * WHY B AND C NEED AN OPERATOR AT ALL, said plainly because it is the honest part. No product path
 637:  * writes `'member'`: the single-seat invariant (AT-001.17) forbids invites, so there is no invite
 638:  * surface to mint a second member and no second seat for one to occupy. The `member` half of the
 639:  * `org_role` enum exists for AT-001.36 and this is how that criterion's Given is reached — the same
 640:  * posture the merged integration-verification item recorded for its own operator-provisioned Givens.
 641:  * The Given is provisioned; the BEHAVIOUR under test is entirely the product's.
 642:  */
 643: async function twoMembershipGiven(
 644:   sut: Awaited<ReturnType<Ctx['open']>>['sut'],
 645:   w: Awaited<ReturnType<Ctx['open']>>['w'],
 646:   label: string,
 647:   names: { a: string; b: string; c: string },
 648: ): Promise<{ session: Session; accountId: string; organizationA: string; organizationB: string; organizationC: string }> {
 649:   const session = await registerConfirmAndSignIn(sut, w.email(label));
 650:   const completion = await sut.completeSignup(
 651:     session,
 652:     { accountType: 'ngo', organizationName: names.a, acknowledgmentTextVersion: TEXT_VERSION },
